  "worked_solution": "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}\u2061x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{\u2218}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{\u2218}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964047/assets/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the values on the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({315}^{\u2218})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, ${315}^{\u2218}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with ${315}^{\u2218}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract ${360}^{\u2218}.$\n\n$$\n{315}^{\u2218}-{360}^{\u2218}=-{45}^{\u2218}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}\u2061(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-{45}^{\u2218}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{arcsin}\u2061(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) & =4\\cdot (-{45}^{\u2218}) \\\\  & ={-180}^{\u2218}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
